piter very  high-resolution I'm going to approximate  it with a rank 400 approximation with  both the deterministic SVD which is  expensive and the randomized SVD which  is cheap and we're going to see how  those compare so running this  reconstruction okay that's going to take  a little bit of time too to multiply all  of these rows and columns and recombine  into X from the SVD and then when that's  done we're going to plot the resulting  reconstruction of Jupiter using these  three these three methods good so here  we have our reconstruction and what you  have here on the left is on the left is  the true image this is the full image of  Jupiter so you can see all this great  detail the Great Red Spot and the kind  of instability region behind it you can  see the deterministic SVD here this is a  rank 400 approximation the very  expensive SVD approximation because  remember computing this SVD of a big  matrix does it is expensive you get away  with less data representation after  computing it so you get a compression  but it's expensive to find that  compression and then over here you see  the randomized SVD approximation and  then again very very faithfully captures  the the true structures in the data so  you can't tell the difference between  the randomized and the deterministic SVD  approximations except that the  randomized SVD was much much much faster  than the deterministic SVD okay and I  want to just go to the code real quick  so the our SVD code here basically takes  the same kind of arguments as the  regular SVD if you take the SVD of a  matrix X and you have some target rank R  ok we tell it what the target rank is so  that's a little different and then there  are these q and p factors which are for  power iterations and for over sampling  so q is the number of power iterations  and P is the over sampling factor and  I'll talk about that in a minute  and so this is all that there is to the  RSV D code it's super simple you  essentially take the data matrix X you  build a random projection matrix P okay  so x times this random projection matrix  P basically samples the column space of  X it multiplies and adds up all of those  columns and random combinations and  makes a skinnier version Z that should  have roughly the same column spaces X  okay so that's what Z equals x times P  is if you have power iterations then you  essentially run through and multiply by  X transpose X again and again and again  q times and that will decrease that'll  make the singular value drop off even  faster and give you better performance  but at the cost of a few more matrix  multiplications and then once we have  this sketched Z matrix which should have  roughly the same column space as X we  compute its QR factorization to get an  orthonormal basis Q we project our data  into that orthonormal basis Q to get  this little teeny tiny Y okay so we  project into Q to get Y and then we  compute the SVD of Y y equals u Sigma V  transpose these are the Y ones and the  Sigma and the V from this SVD are the  same as the Sigma and V from the  original x matrix and the u modes from  our higher dimensional data can be  easily combined as q times these low  dimensional u vectors so really quite a  simple algorithm here only a few lines  of code to compute this very fast  efficient randomized SVD my over  sampling factor P here it tells me if I  want to sample normally you sample our  columns of X through this random  projection matrix you sample our random  projections so this Z is an N by r  matrix and if you over sample you might  sample an extra 5 or 10 columns and you  get better and better performance so you  actually get rapidly faster convergence  of this algorithm even for just a few  extra columns in over sampling  okay so P and Q are kind of these knobs  that you can tune to make your  convergence faster good and then going  back to the code I just wanted to show  what happens if I take a random matrix X  and I compute its SVD so it'll have a  relatively slow singular value drop off  and then I want to show you what power  iterations does to make it have a better  roll-off of energy okay so I take this  big random matrix it's a thousand by a  hundred random matrix so it's it should  be low rank and what we have here are  the singular values at the original data  matrix in white and then what we've done  is essentially this power iteration so  for Q equals one to five we're going to  do one power iteration than another then  another than another and all this means  is we take our data matrix X and we  multiply it by X transpose times X Q  times okay and this over sampling factor  what it does is even after one iteration  of this power iteration the white  singular values which don't decay that  quickly and remember the our SVD relies  on a fast drop off of your singular  value so there really is a low rank  structure then even after one  application of the power iteration our  singular values are much much much much  much lower and they drop off much faster  and then iterating again and again and  again and again gives you this rapid  drop off in your singular value  distribution so this is just a cartoon  that shows you how over how power  iterations forces your singular value  spectrum to look lower rank than it  might actually be so that you could for  example approximate the first ten modes  more accurately using this method okay  so randomized SVD is an extremely  efficient and interesting technique to  compute this the singular value  decomposition when your data does have  low rank structure it's becoming it's a  relatively new technique but it's  becoming a cornerstone of numerical  linear algebra right now so in the  future this is going to be  standard practice if you have low ranked  data to compute the SVD not through the  deterministic method but through the  randomized method you know it has  tunable error bounds because of my  choice of q and p the over sampling  factor and it's really really easy to  code up just a few extra lines of code  to get this randomized SVD okay thank  you

